 "notes": [
  "The tables are trilingual Finnish/Swedish/English; there are no French names, so fr is null throughout.",
  "Table 2 footnote: accepted handwritten candidate lists are counted in the total of valid ballots but not in any party's votes. Their numbers, taken from the 'Kirjoitettuja ehdokaslistoja — Skrivna kandidatlistor' column of table 4 (PDF pages 27-28), are stored under the key 'written' (handwritten candidate lists, not a party); with them the party votes add up to the valid ballots.",
  "Each electoral alliance named its presidential candidate in advance (introduction): Maalaisliitto U. K. Kekkonen (column kekkonen_group), Suomen sosialidemokraattinen puolue K. A. Fagerholm (fagerholm_group), Suomen kansan demokraattinen liitto Eino Kilpi (kilpi_group), Ruotsalainen kansanpuolue R. Törngren (rkp; the column header does not name him, so supports is null), and Suomen kansanpuolue E. Rydman (rydman_group).",
  "Kansallinen kokoomus (kok) and Vapaamielisten liitto (key vapaamieliset, the Liberal Union / De frisinnades förbund) were in an electoral alliance in every constituency with Sakari Tuomioja as their common candidate; table 2 and table 9 print them as two sub-columns under the header 'S. Tuomiojan kannattajat'.",
  "Constituency TOTAL rows are used; they include ballots cast in other constituencies and abroad. The national row of ballots from other constituencies and abroad (77 358) includes 846 votes cast in hospitals (table 2 footnote). Turku north and Kymi have a separate hospital row (Sairaalat) whose votes could not be split by commune.",
  "Changes of constituencies (introduction): the formerly undivided Uusimaa constituency is split into Helsingin kaupungin vaalipiiri and Uudenmaan läänin vaalipiiri, which together elect two more electors than before; both Turku-Pori constituencies, Kuopio east and Vaasa north each lost one elector, and Oulu and Lappi each gained one, compared with 1950.",
  "Electors in table 9 are printed under the heading 'Vaalipiireittäin — Valkretsvis — By electoral areas' (the table has no 'a)' label); the printed total column is used as seats.",
  "Electoral rolls were those of the 1954 parliamentary election plus persons who turned 21 in 1954-1955. Voting in hospitals, at missions abroad and on Finnish ships abroad was possible for the first time (law of 30 June 1955)."
 ],
